selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#why-the-march-is-on-enthalpy-not-temperature\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Why the march is on enthalpy, not temperature<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#why-the-march-is-on-enthalpy-not-temperature\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The obvious way to advance the coolant along a channel is\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(\\Delta T = Q / (\\dot m\\, c_p)\\)</span>, and that is what\n<a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../coupled_solver/CoupledHeatExchangerPhysics.html#pyskyfire.regen.coupled_solver.CoupledHeatExchangerPhysics.coolant_temperature_rate\" title=\"pyskyfire.regen.coupled_solver.CoupledHeatExchangerPhysics.coolant_temperature_rate\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">coolant_temperature_rate()</span></code></a>\ndoes. It is only valid while the coolant is single-phase. Once the bulk\nreaches saturation, added heat goes into latent heat at constant temperature,\nand temperature stops being a state variable at all: inside the dome <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">T</span></code> and\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">p</span></code> are not independent, so <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(T,</span> <span class=\"pre\">p)</span></code> no longer identifies a state.</p><p>That failure is silent rather than loud. CoolProp answers a <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(T,</span> <span class=\"pre\">p)</span></code> query\njust inside the dome by picking a single-phase branch, so a temperature march\nthat steps past <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(T_\\text{sat}\\)</span> lands on <em>superheated vapour</em>. For\nethanol at 20 bar, 0.2 K either side of saturation:</p>", "a[href=\"#module-pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Coolant film properties and two-phase modelling limits for the regenerative march.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties.film_properties\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties.film_properties\">\n<span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">film_properties</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">resolver</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">T_film</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">p</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">getters</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#tuple\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">tuple</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Coolant film properties for the Colburn correlation.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>The film temperature routinely exceeds <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(T_\\text{sat}\\)</span> while the\nbulk is still subcooled liquid, because the wall is hot. A raw <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(T,</span> <span class=\"pre\">p)</span></code>\nlookup there returns <em>vapour</em> properties, which is physically wrong \u2013\nthe fluid at the wall is superheated liquid on the verge of nucleating,\nnot vapour \u2013 and puts a cliff in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">h_cold</span></code> that the local wall solve\ncan fail to cross. Clamping to saturated liquid removes the cliff. It is\nstill not a boiling model: the real coefficient in that region is higher\nthan this returns.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#module-contents\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Module Contents<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-contents\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"#where-the-enthalpy-march-is-unavailable\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Where the enthalpy march is unavailable<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#where-the-enthalpy-march-is-unavailable\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../../thermo/state/index.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.probe_fluid\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.probe_fluid\"><code class=\"xref py py-func docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">probe_fluid()</span></code></a> decides per fluid, once, at solver start:</p>", "a[href=\"../../thermo/state/FluidStateResolver.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidStateResolver\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidStateResolver\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.state.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidStateResolver</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">transport</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">p_ref</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Resolve fluid states from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(h,</span> <span class=\"pre\">p)</span></code>, with cached saturation data.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../coupled_solver/CoupledHeatExchangerPhysics.html#pyskyfire.regen.coupled_solver.CoupledHeatExchangerPhysics.coolant_temperature_rate\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.regen.coupled_solver.CoupledHeatExchangerPhysics.coolant_temperature_rate\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">coolant_temperature_rate</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">T_cool</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">p_cool</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">Q_cold</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Return the coolant temperature change caused by <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Q_cold</span></code> [K].</p><p>Single-phase only \u2013 it assumes all the heat is sensible. Used for\ncoolants that cannot support the enthalpy march (mixtures, and backends\nwithout saturation data); see <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../../thermo/state/index.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.probe_fluid\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.probe_fluid\"><code class=\"xref py py-func docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">probe_fluid()</span></code></a>.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#what-this-module-does-not-model\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">What this module does <em>not</em> model<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#what-this-module-does-not-model\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p><strong>Two-phase heat transfer.</strong> In the dome the coolant-side coefficient is still\nthe single-phase Colburn correlation, evaluated with <em>saturated liquid</em>\nproperties. That is a placeholder in both directions: it misses the flow-boiling\nenhancement (which would raise it, by 2-10x) and it misses dryout (which would\ncollapse it). Treat the quality profile as telling you <em>where</em> the coolant\nboils, not how hot the wall gets there. Nucleate boiling and CHF are not\nmodelled at all.</p><p><strong>Subcooled boiling.</strong> Where the bulk is liquid but the wall is above\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(T_\\text{sat}\\)</span>, real channels boil at the wall. That is not modelled\neither. What <em>is</em> handled is the property-lookup artefact it used to cause: the\nColburn film temperature <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((T_\\text{cool} + T_\\text{cw})/2\\)</span> would cross\nsaturation and CoolProp would return <em>vapour</em> properties for what is physically\nsuperheated liquid, putting a spurious cliff in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">h_cold</span></code>. Film properties are\nnow clamped to saturated liquid instead (see\n<a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties.film_properties\" title=\"pyskyfire.regen.coolant_properties.film_properties\"><code class=\"xref py py-func docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">film_properties()</span></code></a>). Still an approximation, but no longer a\ndiscontinuity, and wrong in the conservative direction.</p>", "a[href=\"#functions\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Functions<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#functions\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"../../thermo/state/FluidStateResolver.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidStateResolver.from_hp\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidStateResolver.from_hp\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">from_hp</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">h</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">p</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../../thermo/state/FluidState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidState</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Resolve the bulk state from specific enthalpy and pressure.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>Inside the dome the temperature is taken as <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">T_sat</span></code> and the density as\nthe homogeneous mixture value, rather than trusting a flash to land on\nthe right branch. Outside it, both come from an <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(h,</span> <span class=\"pre\">p)</span></code> flash, which\nis unambiguous on either side.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../../thermo/state/index.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.probe_fluid\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.probe_fluid\">\n<span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.state.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">probe_fluid</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">fluid</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">p_ref</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../../thermo/state/FluidCapability.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidCapability\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidCapability\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidCapability</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Determine what two-phase support a CoolProp fluid string has.</p><p>Run once per solve, at the inlet pressure. Cheap: a handful of property\ncalls.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>The critical-pressure probe is deliberately tolerant. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Props1SI</span></code> refuses\nmixtures outright, and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AbstractState.p_critical()</span></code> can fail even for\nwell-behaved binaries (ethanol/water reports \u201cfound 4 critical points\u201d), so\na missing critical point downgrades the capability rather than raising.</p></dd>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
